Revenue and gross margin can grow while **each dollar of revenue becomes less profitable**. A shift toward the lower-margin product reduces gross profit per dollar; rising acquisition cost and other operating expenses can then consume more of what remains. Growing gross-margin *dollars* alone would not rule this out—and even a growing gross-margin *percentage* can mask a mix-related drag offset by improvements elsewhere.

The three contributions **can’t be quantified from the information given**. To measure them without double-counting CAC, use this bridge, with all figures expressed as a percentage of revenue:

\[
\Delta\text{contribution margin}
=
\text{product-mix effect}
+\text{other gross-margin changes}
-\Delta\text{acquisition spend ratio}
-\Delta\text{other opex ratio}.
\]

CAC-related spend must be separated from other opex if it is already included in the reported opex ratio. The change in acquisition spend as a share of revenue also cannot be inferred from the change in **CAC per customer** alone; it depends on how many customers were acquired and how much revenue they generated.

**Illustration, not an estimate for your business:** Suppose gross margin rises from 50% to 52%, but a mix shift costs 2 percentage points and other gross-margin improvements add 4 points. Meanwhile, acquisition spend rises from 5% to 7% of revenue and other opex from 20% to 23%. Contribution margin falls from **25% to 22%**, despite higher gross margin. Of the **7 points of adverse pressure before offsets**, mix accounts for **2/7 (29%)**, acquisition spend **2/7 (29%)**, and other opex **3/7 (43%)**; the 4-point improvement elsewhere in gross margin offsets part of that pressure.

For a true customer-level unit-economics answer, run the same bridge by acquisition cohort, comparing contribution profit or lifetime value with CAC. That distinguishes a genuine deterioration per customer from an opex ratio rising because of growth investment or timing.
